<TopicPage slug="subjects/history/intermediate/the-history-of-science">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    The history of science asks <strong>how humans learned to learn about nature reliably</strong>
    — a question with a surprisingly short answer: mostly since 1600. What changed wasn't
    intelligence (Aristotle was no fool) but method and institutions: experiments, mathematics,
    publishing, and the social contract that lets a rival check your work. The story explains
    both why science works and why it took civilisations so long to build it — the answer to
    the second being the real content of the field: a working system of knowledge needs more
    than brilliant individuals; it needs instruments that extend the senses, mathematics that
    compresses observation, publication that makes claims checkable, and institutions that
    reward the checkers. This page follows that construction through four transformations —
    the method revolution of the 1600s, Newton's synthesis, the professionalisation of the
    1800s and the big science of the post-1945 world — then examines the themes the history
    keeps teaching: how revolutions actually happen, what instruments do to discovery, the
    real (complicated) relationship with religion, and the question of who gets counted.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="four transformations, from philosophy to big science" widget="equationStep" config={sciCfg} />

  <h2>Natural philosophy: what the world looked like before</h2>
  <p>
    Before 1600, the dominant way of knowing nature was <strong>authority plus
    reason</strong>: Aristotle's physics (heavy bodies seek their natural place;
    the heavens are perfect and unchanging), Ptolemy's astronomy (Earth
    central, spheres carrying planets), Galen's medicine (humours to balance)
    — each internally coherent, each taught in universities for centuries,
    each defended by argument from texts. The system was not stupid: it
    explained, predicted and organised. Its weakness was structural —
    <strong>knowledge did not compound</strong>. A disagreement was settled by
    citing authorities or spinning syllogisms; experiments were occasional
    and disreputable ("mechanical arts" were for artisans); mathematics
    described the heavens but not the sublunar world. Islamic and Indian
    traditions had already run ahead on parts of the programme —
    al-Biruni's measurement, Ibn al-Haytham's optics, the Kerala
    school's series for π — and the 1600s built a <em>delivery
    system</em> for exactly that kind of work. That distinction —
    between good ideas and a system that generates and tests them
    — is the field's central insight.
  </p>

  <h2>The method revolution, 1543–1687</h2>
  <p>
    The transformation came as a package, and it is worth seeing each piece.
    <strong>The instrument:</strong> the telescope (Dutch spectacle-makers,
    1608) reached Galileo within a year — his 1610 <em>Sidereus Nuncius</em>
    reported moons around Jupiter, mountains on the Moon, phases of Venus:
    the heavens were neither perfect nor Earth-centred, <em>and anyone with
    a tube could check</em>. The microscope opened a second invisible world
    (Leeuwenhoek's "animalcules", the 1670s–80s). <strong>The measurement:</strong>
    Galileo's inclined planes (1638) replaced authority about falling bodies
    with numbers — slowing the fall so a water clock could time it, finding
    distance growing with the square of time: nature interrogated in
    mathematics. <strong>The method statement:</strong> Francis Bacon's
    <em>Novum Organum</em> (1620) argued for systematic experiment and
    induction — knowledge as a collective project, not a private wisdom.
    <strong>The institution:</strong> the <strong>Royal Society (1660)</strong> and
    the French Académie des Sciences (1666) made publishing and
    priority-sharing a norm — the Society's motto, <em>nullius in verba</em>
    ("take nobody's word for it"), is the whole programme in three words.
    <strong>The mathematics:</strong> Descartes' coordinate geometry (1637)
    and Newton and Leibniz's calculus turned motion itself into algebra.
    Each piece had precedents; the novelty was their <em>assembly</em>
    into a cumulative machine.
  </p>

  <h2>Newton's synthesis: the sky becomes physics</h2>
  <p>
    The <strong><em>Principia</em> (1687)</strong> is the system's first masterpiece. Newton
    took Kepler's empirical laws of planetary motion (elliptical orbits,
    1609–19), Galileo's terrestrial mechanics, and his own three laws and
    universal gravitation — one inverse-square law explaining falling
    apples, tides, and orbiting moons alike. The unification's audacity is
    the point: the <em>same</em> mathematics governs heaven and earth, a
    claim so radical that even Newton's continental readers resisted it
    (action at a distance seemed occult) until it predicted everything it
    touched — Halley's comet's return (1758, predicted on the theory), the
    Earth's oblate shape, the precession of the equinoxes. The cultural
    aftershock was as important: if nature ran on discoverable law, then
    law was there to be read — the Enlightenment's confidence
    (<em>"Nature and Nature's laws lay hid in night: / God said, Let Newton
    be! and all was light"</em>, Pope's epitaph) was built on the
    <em>Principia</em>'s success. The lesson every later science
    learned: a good theory does not just explain — it
    <em>commands belief by predicting what nobody has yet
    measured</em>.
  </p>

  <h2>Professionalisation: science becomes a career (1800s)</h2>
  <p>
    The nineteenth century rebuilt science as an institution — the very word
    "scientist" was coined by Whewell only in 1833. The university
    laboratory arrived: <strong>Liebig's chemistry lab at Giessen</strong> (from
    1826) trained students in cohorts, and the German research-university
    model — <strong>PhD training, seminars, professors with institutes</strong> —
    spread worldwide. Journals professionalised (peer review hardened
    through the century); <strong>government funding</strong> arrived (the
    1840s Kew and Geological Survey models; Germany's Kaiser-Wilhelm
    institutes, 1911); and industry hired science — the German dye and
    electrical industries grew <em>out of</em> university chemistry and
    physics, the clearest early proof that research was an economic
    engine (the second industrial revolution: chemicals, steel,
    electricity, telephony). The period's sciences set patterns still
    current: big classification projects (Darwin's voyage was a state-
    funded survey; Darwinian evolution, 1859, ran on decades of
    professional naturalism), quantification as the mark of a real
    discipline (thermodynamics, Maxwell's electromagnetic equations —
    1865 — the second great unification), and the laboratory as a
    machine for producing discoveries. Science stopped being what
    gentlemen did and became what careers were made of — with
    everything that implies about who could enter.
  </p>

  <h2>Big science: research at scale (1945→)</h2>
  <p>
    The Second World War merged science with the state. The <strong>Manhattan
    Project</strong> — some 130,000 people, several secret cities, one goal —
    demonstrated that organised research could move history; <strong>radar</strong>
    (the MIT Radiation Laboratory: thousands of physicists, centimetre-
    wave engineering that arguably won the Battle of the Atlantic) built
    the electronics industry's pedigree. After Sputnik (1957), the Cold War
    funded the pattern permanently: <strong>NASA</strong>, the NSF, the national
    laboratories, <strong>CERN</strong> (founded 1954; the Large Hadron
    Collider's international teams run to thousands of authors per
    paper). "Big science" changed the sociology — team credit,
    facility politics, instrumentation budgets larger than theory
    careers — but not the epistemology: the LHC's discovery papers
    still rest on the 1660s contract of publishing and testing, now
    executed by collaborations the size of towns. The through-line
    of all four eras is worth stating plainly: the Scientific
    Revolution invented the <em>habit</em> (test, publish, correct),
    the 1800s built the <em>career</em>, the 1900s built the
    <em>budget</em> — and the habit survived all three.
  </p>

  <h2>How revolutions actually happen</h2>
  <p>
    The field's most influential modern book, Thomas Kuhn's <strong>The
    Structure of Scientific Revolutions (1962)</strong>, gave the vocabulary:
    normal science works inside a <strong>paradigm</strong> until anomalies
    accumulate, then a crisis resolves in a shift to a new framework
    that redefines the questions. Historians have complicated the neat
    version — revolutions are <em>gradual, contested and local</em>.
    <strong>Copernicus (1543)</strong> was received as a better calendar
    calculation, not a cosmological manifesto; heliocentrism needed a
    century of refinement (Kepler's ellipses, Galileo's telescope,
    Newton's mechanics) to become irresistible. <strong>Darwin (1859)</strong>
    persuaded through a mountain of professional evidence — and took
    decades to settle into consensus. <strong>Einstein (1905/1915)</strong>
    won relativity by prediction (the 1919 eclipse starlight bending)
    rather than revolution-by-fiat. The pattern the history actually
    shows: <strong>new instruments, new mathematics, and a young
    generation with careers to make</strong> — each shift accelerated
    by the same three accelerants, and each argued out in
    journals rather than decided by any single genius
    moment.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> science advanced by great men having
  flashes of genius. The record shows systems: Newton's calculus was
  invented simultaneously by Leibniz; Darwin co-published with Wallace;
  the double helix ran on Franklin's X-ray data and a lab ecosystem.
  Genius is real and unevenly distributed — but the <em>engine</em> is the
  institutional machinery that lets a hundred workers convert a
  hunch into a checked result, and the machinery, not the flashes,
  is what was historically rare.</p>

  <h2>Print: the method's first delivery system</h2>
  <p>
    Before the telescope, the revolution needed a press. <strong>Movable-type
    printing</strong> (Gutenberg, c. 1450) transformed what a claim could be:
    identical copies meant that errors were <em>shared and correctable</em>
    rather than unique and drifting; diagrams and tables reproduced
    exactly (Euclid's geometrical figures had been hand-copied into
    guesswork for a millennium); and price collapsed — a library that
    a royal collector had hoarded became a scholar's shelf. The
    scientific literature's habits grew in this medium: title pages
    claiming priority, errata lists correcting shared mistakes, and
    the sixteenth century's technical book trades (Aldus Manutius's
    algebra editions, the 1543 publication of both Copernicus and
    Vesalius — anatomy rebuilt on printed plates) created the
    market that the Royal Society's <em>Philosophical Transactions</em>
    (1665, still publishing) organised into a system. The
    modern parallels write themselves — the web did to print
    what print did to manuscript, with the same mix of
    reach and noise — which is why historians of science
    read the printing revolution as the field's first
    information-technology chapter.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Religion and science: beyond the war story.</strong> The Victorian
    "conflict thesis" (Draper 1874, White 1896) has been largely retired:
    the Church <em>funded</em> astronomy (calendar reform engaged Copernicus's
    work; Gregorian reform, 1582, was a research programme), universities
    were ecclesiastical institutions, clergy were over-represented among
    naturalists (Darwin trained for the Church; Mendel was a friar), and
    the Galileo affair — genuinely shameful — was a specific collision of
    politics, personality and biblical over-literalism rather than proof
    of a general war. The honest model is <strong>negotiation with local
    politics</strong>: periods of synergy (natural theology funding
    geology), specific flashpoints (1633; Huxley versus Wilberforce,
    1860), and a modern settlement where the institutions largely
    separate their questions.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Who gets counted.</strong> The field keeps widening its frame, and
    each widening changes the story. Rosalind Franklin's X-ray
    crystallography was essential to the double helix structure (1953)
    while her name was not on the credit it deserved; the women
    "computers" of Harvard's observatory and NASA's calculators did
    the quantitative backbone of astronomy and spaceflight; and
    non-Western science — the Islamic Golden Age's optics and
    algebra, Chinese invention (paper, printing, the compass),
    Indian mathematics — is now written <em>into</em> the narrative
    rather than footnoted after it. The newest frame is the
    history of failure and replication: priority disputes,
    error correction and the replication crisis are not
    embarrassments to the story but the machinery working —
    the field's self-correction is its defining feature,
    which is why the history of science and the
    philosophy of science have merged.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Open questions.</strong> Why did the delivery system assemble in
    Europe in the 1600s — printing, voyages, war-driven instrument
    demand, Protestant reading culture, colonial botany? (The debate
    is live and contested.) Does the big-science model still deliver
    per dollar (the "science is getting harder" argument — are low-
    hanging fruits exhausted)? And can the checking habit scale to
    machine-generated science, where the volume of claims outruns
    any referee pool? The 1600s answered "how do we know" with
    "let everyone check" — the next chapter is whether the
    checking can keep up.
  </p>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Kuhn, The Structure of Scientific Revolutions (1962); Wootton, The Invention of Science (2015); Shapin, The Scientific Revolution; Lindberg, The Beginnings of Western Science.</p>

